; Same fish-base vertex as flatten-opposite-ray-toward-{b,d}.bel, but two of
; the three given rays are PINNED (`.b valley`, `.a mountain`) instead of
; left bare. Pinning narrows the whole 4-ray Maekawa search from 8 patterns
; to 2 (the two still-free rays — --l2 & .d and the emergent ray — must now
; share polarity to keep |M-V|=2). Kawasaki/candidate-geometry never depends
; on M/V, so the same two line-new candidates that fail for EVERY pattern in
; the bare case (see the sibling files) still fail for both of these; only
; the diagonal's OppositeRay continuation (O -> c, the bare case's own
; completion) is a candidate at all, and of its 2 Maekawa-consistent
; patterns, only ONE seats in-bounds (the other self-intersects/folds off
; the paper — an M/V-dependent property, unlike Kawasaki). So the pool this
; statement pools from already has exactly one member — |S| = 1 before the
; three-stage selection is even reached, and `{toward}` is redundant.
;
; Verified by hand (not asserted here — `.bel` has no rank/stacking
; vocabulary, and `faces` alone can't distinguish "no ambiguity" from
; "toward broke a tie"): adding `{toward .b}`, `{toward .d}`, or even
; `{toward .c}` (the ac-diagonal symmetry axis, which errors with "does not
; pick a side" whenever |S| > 1) all commit to the byte-identical fold as
; this toward-less statement.

paper square

mark --diag = map .a onto .c
mark --ray = through .a .c

step left
mark --l1 = map --ab onto --diag
mark --l2 = map --da onto --diag
flatten (--l1 & .b valley) (--l2 & .d) (--ray & .a mountain)

; assert faces = 6
